Ground rules — these come before any work:

- **Never invent work.** If you do not have explicit direction (a stated
  task, an issue/MR target, or concrete instructions), ask with
  AskUserQuestion and wait for the answer. What an unanswered question
  timeout MEANS depends on where the run is:
  - **Pre-plan** (initial task definition, brainstorming, design/spec/plan
    approval): a timed-out question means STOP AND WAIT — record
    `work state set --stop-reason=question --question "<the question>"`
    and end your turn. The user will find the open question via the
    resume brief. Never proceed on a guess.
  - **Post-plan / resume context** (direction already recorded, picking up
    where a previous session left off): you may proceed on your best
    judgment after the timeout.
- **Once direction is clear, record it before starting:**
  `work goal "<one-line statement of the task>"`. A run with work happening
  and no goal recorded is a contract violation, not a shortcut. Include an
  estimate when you can size the work —
  `work goal "..." --estimate-sessions 2 --estimate-time "3h"` — it is
  compared against the actuals at completion.
- **Name the run once direction is clear:** derive a short, memorable
  kebab-case name from the task and record it with `work name <name>`.
  Do NOT ask the user to confirm the name — pick it yourself and move
  on (the user can rename at any time with `work name <new-name>`;
  auto-slugs are unfindable later). Skip this if the resume brief
  already shows a name.
- **HARD RULE — commit before every stop that awaits the human.** Every
  stop that awaits the human — a blocking question, a phasic yield, an
  approval gate — must be preceded by `work commit`: unpushed artifacts
  are invisible to the reviewer.

Contract — keep the state truthful as you work. State updates are part of
the work, not optional bookkeeping:

- Record each phase transition BEFORE starting the phase's work:
  `work state set --phase=<name>` — treat an unrecorded phase as not
  started.
- Every phase END is a pushed, linked deliverable: run `work commit` before
  the transition and cite the deliverable's web URL (the `Run dir:` link
  from the resume brief) in your report — never a container path.
- BEFORE stopping to ask the user a blocking question: record the question
  text too — `work state set --stop-reason=question --question "<the question>"`
  — then `work commit`, then END the session immediately. Never idle in-session
  waiting for an answer: the next session's resume brief surfaces the recorded
  question first. Answers arrive via a FRESH session — the human relaunches
  with `lmer ... --answer "<text>"` (or records `work answer "<text>"`) and
  that session resumes seeded with the question+answer pair; never revive
  the asking session.
- At a phasic phase-end yield: `work state set --stop-reason=yield`
- On completion: `work state set --status=complete --stop-reason=complete`
- Only for a genuine unrecoverable failure: `work state set --stop-reason=critical_error --critical-error '{"summary": "...", "detail": "..."}'`
- To resume work on the run (e.g. it was marked complete but new work arrived): `work state set --status=in-progress --stop-reason=none`
- Persist durable artifacts (specs, plans, retros): `work artifact <name>.md --file <path>`.
  Specs, plans, and reports deliver as Markdown (`.md`) — never docx/pdf/binary
  documents (unreviewable, undiffable, unlinkable at line level).
- Log notable moments: `work event <type> --note "..."`
- **Prove "done" with receipts.** Validation only counts when a tool
  recorded it: the gate commands (`gate-check`/`gate-commit`/`gate-push`)
  write gate receipts automatically; for any other validation command run
  it through `work verify <name> -- <command …>` (streams the output,
  mirrors the exit code, records a `verify` receipt). Claiming a plan task
  complete requires a matching `verify`/`gate` receipt from the current
  session — a "done" with no receipt is prose, not proof.
- **Plans carry validation contracts.** Give each task in plan.md a
  `verify: <command or gate>` line naming the command that proves it, and
  use that name/command when recording the receipt.
- **Multi-task plans carry a machine-checkable index.** When plan.md has
  more than one task, author `plan.index.json` beside it (schema 1: per
  task `id`, `description`, `files` — the declared write-scope as
  project-relative paths/globs, `deps`, `verify_commands`, and
  `session_scope: "one"`, or `"multi"` plus a `scope_rationale` saying in
  writing why it cannot fit one session). Genuinely shared touchpoints
  (CHANGELOG.yaml, docs indexes) between independent tasks go in a
  top-level `shared_files` allowlist (`{"T2+T4": ["CHANGELOG.yaml"]}`).
  Register it: `work artifact plan.index.json --file <path>`.
- **The plan gate requires a green `work plan check`.** Run the lint
  BEFORE presenting plan.md for approval and include its output in the
  approval request — the human approves a plan whose task DAG is checked
  acyclic, whose independent tasks touch disjoint paths, and whose
  atomicity declarations are complete. A failing check blocks the plan
  gate: fix the plan (or declare the shared touchpoint) and re-run until
  green before asking.
- **Goals are a frozen contract.** For runs that produce a spec, draft
  `goals.md` beside it during the interview/brainstorm phase — masterplan
  format: `## G<N>: <title>` headings, each active goal carrying
  `signal:` (one of `test|command|artifact|docs`) and `evidence:` naming
  its evidence source; lint drafts with `work goals check`. The moment the
  spec is approved, freeze the set:
  `work goals freeze --note "<approval context>"` — approving the spec IS
  the freeze. Any later change to goals.md goes through
  `work goals amend` (removed goals become tombstones, ids never
  renumber) — never a silent edit; an unamended edit surfaces as
  divergence at finish. `work plan check` warns on goals no plan task
  covers.
- **Assess goals before declaring the run complete.** BEFORE
  `work state set --status=complete`, run `work goals assess` (bare form
  prints the skeleton), then record the verdicts:
  `work goals assess --verdict 'G<N>=<met|partial|missed|waived>:<evidence>'`
  — one flag per active goal, evidence citing a receipt or registered
  artifact where possible (free prose is allowed but marked) — and land
  the printed verdict table in retro.md. Nudge-don't-block: assess never
  stops you, but a completed run with no assessment is a gap the reviewer
  will flag.
- **Ledger every landed task in the same breath as its commit.** The moment
  a gate-commit lands a plan task, record it before doing anything else:
  `work ledger set <task-id> --status done --commit <sha> [--receipt <name>]`.
  Task ids come from plan.md (hand-named ids are fine); `work ledger set`
  is the ONLY writer of the task↔commit mapping — gates never write it.
  Mark a task `in-progress` when you start it, `deferred`/`dropped` when it
  leaves scope. A crashed run's successor recovers by reading `work ledger`
  — an unledgered commit is invisible to it.
